From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mark Harfouche <mark.harfouche@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 5 Sep 2026 14:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Remove tf.compat.v1 from the pose engine's inference,
 training and export

core/predict.py, core/train.py, predict_videos.py and export.py were the last
large block of TensorFlow 1 in the engine: 44 tf.compat.v1 references, 8
Sessions, 6 placeholders, and the tf.compat.v1.disable_eager_execution() call
at core/train.py line 34 that ran at *import* and so turned eager execution off
for the whole process, for everyone.  That one line is why the ported Keras
networks could not be reached at runtime: setup_pose_prediction wrapped them in
a Session with a name-based Saver.

Those four files now contain no tf.compat.v1, no Session and no placeholder.
Everything that was TF1 in them has been moved, unchanged, into one new module,
core/legacy_tf1.py, which is entered only when the tf1 engine is selected and
is meant to be deleted whole once the Keras nets are the only implementation.
The disable_eager_execution() call goes with it, and now runs from
ensure_graph_mode() at the top of the TF1 entry points instead of at import, so
importing deeplabcut leaves eager execution on.

  core/eager_session.py  EagerSession, the (sess, inputs, outputs) contract
                         backed by a tf.function.  That contract is a duck
                         type, not a Session -- DeepLabCut already ships a
                         second implementation of it in
                         core/openvino/session.py -- so predict_videos.py,
                         core/evaluate.py, core/evaluate_multianimal.py,
                         visualizemaps.py and core/test.py need no changes.
  core/legacy_tf1.py     the TF1 engine, in one file, for deletion.

The engine is chosen with nnets/factory.resolve_engine, i.e. tf_engine in
pose_cfg.yaml or DLC_TF_ENGINE, and tf1 is still the default, so a stock
project is unaffected.

The checkpoint plumbing is nnets/checkpoint's; the eager training helpers are
core/train_eager's.  core/train.py re-exports LearningRate from train_eager,
which had to keep its own copy only because importing core/train.py used to
disable eager execution, and re-exports the TF1 input queue and optimiser from
legacy_tf1 for anything still importing them from here.

Inference.  setup_pose_prediction and setup_GPUpose_prediction build the Keras
net, restore the snapshot by name and hand back an EagerSession.  The inference
heads needed no migration: BasePoseNet.test and BasePoseNet.inference are
already plain TensorFlow 2 and are inherited unchanged by the Keras nets, so
they are called rather than rewritten.  Against the tf_slim Session, on 16 real
480x640 frames:

    resnet_50, batch 1        part_prob 2.7e-07  locref 8.3e-06
    resnet_50, batch 8        part_prob 3.3e-07  locref 7.6e-06
    resnet_50 +PAF, batch 1   part_prob 2.9e-07  locref 3.2e-06  paf 3.9e-06
    resnet_50 +PAF, batch 8   part_prob 3.0e-07  locref 3.0e-06  paf 3.9e-06
    MuNet, stride 4           part_prob 3.7e-07  locref 4.4e-06  paf 6.0e-06

The peak-index tensor is bit-identical in every case (343, 248 and 497-552
peaks per frame), the keypoints predict.getpose / getposeNP decode agree to
1.0e-05 px, and the "pose" tensor of setup_GPUpose_prediction to 3.1e-05 px.
Over a whole 2330-frame video, analyze_videos moves keypoints by at most
6.1e-05 px, mean 2.0e-06 px, and evaluate_network reports the same errors to
the two decimals it prints: 26.9 px train / 32.23 px test, 7.02 / 7.43 with the
p-cutoff.  Running the same video through the tf1 engine after this patch
reproduces the unpatched package bit for bit.

Training.  slim.learning.create_train_op becomes train_eager.make_train_step,
the FIFOQueue with its thread and Coordinator becomes train_eager.make_dataset,
and the Saver becomes train_eager.SnapshotSaver, so the single-animal loop and
core/train_multianimal.py replace create_train_op the same way.

create_train_op made every op in GraphKeys.UPDATE_OPS a control dependency of
the loss, which is what kept the batch-norm moving averages trained.  Measured
on the real DeepLabCut training graphs:

    resnet_50           53 batch-norm nodes, all is_training=False,   0 update ops
    resnet_50 +PAF      53 batch-norm nodes, all is_training=False,   0 update ops
    mobilenet_v2_1.0    52 batch-norm nodes, all is_training=True,  104 update ops
    MuNet, stride 4     58 batch-norm nodes, 5 is_training=True,     10 update ops

with every update op reachable from the train op.  Keras batch-norm applies
that update inside the layer call when it is called with is_training=True, so
12 steps of the ported loop on the Keras MuNet move all 10 of the decoder's
moving_mean/moving_variance tensors and leave all 106 of the backbone's frozen
-- which is the other half of the check, because multi.py builds the resnet
backbone is_training=False even while training and the port has to reproduce
that too.

Checked step for step against create_train_op from the same snapshot over the
same real batches, 8 steps at lr 1e-4, over all 269 (resnet) / 271 (+PAF)
variables:

    momentum   loss 3.7e-09   weights max 1.5e-08, mean 1.8e-13, none over 1e-5
    adam       loss 6.1e-07   weights max 2.1e-04, mean 1.1e-07

The momentum figures are roundoff.  Adam amplifies it, because its first steps
divide by sqrt(v)+epsilon where both are near zero; for scale, running the
unmodified TF1 code twice with oneDNN on and off diverges by 5.8e-04 max and
3.4e-06 mean over the same eight steps, so the port sits nearer the reference
run than two legitimate TF1 runs sit to each other.  Two differences that are
not roundoff were found: the TF1 AdamOptimizer defaults to epsilon 1e-8 where
keras.optimizers.legacy.Adam defaults to 1e-7, worth 9.3e-04 in eight steps
(train_eager.make_optimizer passes it), and DeepLabCut never sums the
regularization-losses collection into the loss -- 37 to 70 dead weight-decay
terms depending on net_type -- so model.losses must stay out of the tape.

Snapshots are written by train_eager.SnapshotSaver under the tf_slim variable
names, so they are ordinary DeepLabCut snapshots: one written by the eager
trainer and restored into the unmodified tf_slim graph reproduces the eager
engine's own predictions to 2.2e-07 on part_prob and 1.6e-05 on locref.

Export.  Freezing an eager model names its nodes after Keras layers, so none of
the names DLC-Live loads by survive -- of 3796 TF1 op names exactly one does.
Wrapping each output in tf.identity with its old name inside the traced
function restores all of them.  Exporting the same model through
deeplabcut.export_model with each engine and loading the .pb by node name, the
way DLC-Live does:

    multi-animal   Sigmoid, pose/locref_pred/block4/BiasAdd,
                   pose/pairwise_pred/block4/BiasAdd  all bit-identical,
                   Cast_2 identical
    single animal  Sigmoid 2.4e-07, pose/locref_pred/block4/BiasAdd 5.7e-06
    TFGPUinference concat_1 bit-identical

that is, the three multi-animal outputs the earlier patch added included.  The
input node is still called Placeholder, which is what export.py reads.

predict_videos.py loses its four reset_default_graph() calls to
tf.keras.backend.clear_session(), which the file already used at line 223 and
which resets the TF1 default graph as well as the eager Keras state, and
GetPoseF_GTF now flips and reshapes the pose array in numpy instead of
appending tf.gather and tf.reshape to the inference graph, so the pose network
is the only thing in the file that builds TensorFlow ops.

Not fixed here, because the files belong elsewhere: nnets/base.py's train()
still calls tf.compat.v1.losses (5 references) and prediction_layers still
opens a variable_scope, and the efficientnet and mobilenet backbones are
unported.  Excluding legacy_tf1.py, which exists to be deleted, this leaves 72
tf.compat.v1 references in the tree, none of them in the engine's entry points.

Prerequisites: this applies on top of the ResNet/MuNet Keras port (it uses
nnets/factory's engine selection and nnets/checkpoint) and the multi-animal
training port (it uses core/train_eager).  It touches no file any other patch
in the series touches.
---
diff --git a/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/core/eager_session.py b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/core/eager_session.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1e6fa5c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/deeplabcut/pose_estimation_tensorflow/core/eager_session.py
@@ -0,0 +1,104 @@
+#
+# DeepLabCut Toolbox (deeplabcut.org)
+# © A. & M.W. Mathis Labs
+# https://github.com/DeepLabCut/DeepLabCut
+#
